		{"id":"39973913f7b07c7d","type":"group","x":6540,"y":-160,"width":1067,"height":320,"label":"Evaluation and Readout"},
		{"id":"01066344b3b34b81","type":"text","text":"# Mermaid Map of Methods solving for Electronic Hamiltonian\n```mermaid\ngraph TD\n\n    %% Style Definitions\n\n    classDef operator fill:#f9f,stroke:#333,stroke-width:2px;\n\n    classDef math fill:#bbf,stroke:#333,stroke-width:1px;\n\n    classDef physical fill:#bfb,stroke:#333,stroke-width:1px;\n\n    %% Core Electronic Hamiltonian\n\n    H_elec[Electronic Hamiltonian Operator] -->|Solved via Quantum Mechanics| SE[Schrödinger Equation]\n\n    %% Branching into the two conceptual layers\n\n    SE --> MeanField[1\\. One-Electron Approximation <br> e.g., Hartree-Fock]\n\n    SE --> Exact[2\\. Many-Electron Reality <br> e.g., Full CI]\n\n  \n\n    %% One-Electron Layer\n\n    MeanField --> Fock[Fock Matrix / One-Electron Hamiltonian]\n\n    Fock -->|Diagonalization| OEigen[Eigen Problem]\n\n    OEigen -->|Eigenvalues| OEnergies[Orbital Energies <br> HOMO, LUMO, etc.]\n\n    OEigen -->|Eigenvectors| OShapes[Molecular Orbitals <br> Spatial Wavefunctions / Shapes]\n\n    %% Many-Electron Layer\n\n    Exact --> HMatrix[Full Hamiltonian Matrix]\n\n    OEnergies & OShapes -->|Combined into| Configurations[Slater Determinants <br> Orbital Configurations]\n\n    Configurations --> HMatrix\n\n    HMatrix -->|Diagonalization| MEigen[Eigen Problem]\n\n    MEigen -->|Eigenvalues| StateE[Total Molecular Energy <br> Ground & Excited States]\n\n    MEigen -->|Eigenvectors| StateWave[Many-Electron Wavefunction <br> State Eigenbasis Mix]\n\n  \n\n    %% Connecting back to Chemistry\n\n    OEnergies --> PhysicalArrangement[ORBITAL ARRANGEMENT & SPECTROSCOPY]\n\n    OShapes --> PhysicalArrangement\n\n    StateWave --> ChemicalBehavior[CHEMICAL REACTIVITY & GEOMETRY]\n\n    StateE --> ChemicalBehavior\n\n  \n\n    %% Applying Classes\n\n    class H_elec,Fock,HMatrix operator;\n\n    class OEigen,MEigen,SE math;\n\n    class OEnergies,OShapes,StateE,StateWave,PhysicalArrangement,ChemicalBehavior physical;\n```\n","x":-780,"y":-4108,"width":1180,"height":1680},
		{"id":"28732cfcfc7325ec","type":"file","file":"pages/chemistry/Methods for the Electronic Hamiltonian.md","x":-599,"y":-2238,"width":820,"height":900},
		{"id":"95cf4b50dfb4c4ac","type":"file","file":"pages/chemistry/Electronic Structure Problem.md","x":-1800,"y":-2148,"width":923,"height":720},
		{"id":"de2804ee3a1629c7","type":"file","file":"media-attachments/Pasted image 20260602083252.png","x":-2582,"y":-100,"width":782,"height":230},
		{"id":"6ec17e1bfe742169","type":"text","text":"# Protein folding","x":-2585,"y":140,"width":265,"height":60},
		{"id":"a0b9d01853144473","type":"file","file":"pages/chemistry/Minimal Basis Sets.md","x":-519,"y":-1068,"width":659,"height":340},
		{"id":"a50c92e62d6e4777","type":"file","file":"pages/chemistry/Chemistry Basis Set.md","x":-1800,"y":-1308,"width":923,"height":740},
		{"id":"13c7a2ec23b55a4d","type":"file","file":"pages/chemistry/Hamiltonian.md","x":-2880,"y":-1928,"width":688,"height":280},
		{"id":"9684a51b07a5051b","type":"file","file":"pages/chemistry/Solving Eigenstates.md","x":-2780,"y":-1563,"width":520,"height":510},
		{"id":"d8f095e6c4567f25","type":"text","text":"# Optimization Problem: \nSet up a cost Hamiltonian H_cost whose ground state encodes the minimum-energy conformation. QAOA and VQE are your solvers. At low circuit depth QAOA doesn't beat classical. At high depth it increasingly approximates Grover and gets fractional speedup.","x":-2366,"y":480,"width":566,"height":240},
		{"id":"8c538aa7d7562042","type":"text","text":"# Search problem\nThe database is all possible conformers. The marked item is the lowest-energy one. Grover gives you O(√N) vs O(N) classical. Optimality is proven — Grover defines a geodesic in quantum Hilbert space, so any other quantum search algorithm is just a parametrized approximation of it.","x":-2366,"y":240,"width":566,"height":220},
		{"id":"f668bf8055935915","type":"file","file":"media-attachments/Pasted image 20260602085650.png","x":-3180,"y":720,"width":682,"height":360},
		{"id":"23033b6bb71db598","type":"text","text":"Comparing this to the single-atom case:1 Hydrogen atom ($N=10, E=1$): $\\binom{10}{1} = 10$ configurations.1 Hydrogen molecule ($N=10, E=2$): $\\binom{10}{2} = 45$ configurations.","x":3960,"y":-4340,"width":760,"height":100},
		{"id":"7216579fd61aa44f","type":"file","file":"pages/chemistry/Quantum Chemistry Challenges.md","x":4000,"y":-2396,"width":520,"height":336},
		{"id":"2dda5195d537a7a6","type":"text","text":"# Discretized Search Space\nThe conformational search space is 3^(2(n-1)) for a protein of length n (each of n-1 peptide bonds has two flanking dihedral angles φ and ψ, each discretized to 3 states).","x":-2366,"y":810,"width":566,"height":180},
		{"id":"1ef4d29e0c8c0854","type":"text","text":"TC10b Miniprotein (Trp-Cage Variant)In computational biology and protein folding research, TC10b is a highly studied, artificially engineered variant of the Trp-cage miniprotein.\n\n It is one of the smallest known functional proteins. TC10b was designed to have a more stable alpha-helical structure compared to the original Trp-cage variant (TC5b).\n\nBecause of its rapid folding speed and high thermal stability, researchers frequently use it to model how proteins fold and unfold in computer simulations and experiments.","x":-2460,"y":1300,"width":621,"height":284},
		{"id":"b258864c784b3081","type":"file","file":"media-attachments/Pasted image 20260602090902.png","x":-2614,"y":2260,"width":841,"height":252},
		{"id":"f3907f726fad4e46","type":"file","file":"pages/chemistry/Fermion-to-Qubit Mappings.md","x":5005,"y":-2980,"width":948,"height":400},
		{"id":"55fe6b5fa878befc","type":"text","text":"# Ruggedness\nRuggedness of the energy landscape is the key indicator of quantum advantage. More rugged = more local minima = more uncertainty about the search space = larger quantum speedup. This is the \"worst case\" in algorithmic complexity analysis, which is exactly where quantum shines. Quantum tunneling lets the algorithm cross energy barriers probabilistically with no classical analog.","x":-1680,"y":348,"width":568,"height":265},
		{"id":"1432ffb58fc026e5","type":"text","text":"# Classical Ab Initio Methods\n\nFigure 3 in the paper shows this concretely — at 100 residues you're looking at 10^95 conformations","x":-1680,"y":810,"width":566,"height":180},
		{"id":"16ec7d5baa1b9a49","type":"file","file":"pages/chemistry/Ground State Problem.md","x":5220,"y":-2368,"width":517,"height":280},
		{"id":"4c5a71419fc81684","type":"file","file":"pages/chemistry/Solving for Singular Hydrogen.md","x":4880,"y":-5180,"width":1220,"height":1720},
		{"id":"02bb45ea00039966","type":"text","text":"Dihydrogen Monoxide ($H_2O$)The Setup: 10 electrons. \n\nUsing the 6-31G(d) basis set from our previous context, we have $N = 26$ spin-orbitals.\n\nQubits Required: 26 Qubits.\n\n# Jordan-Wigner: \nTo simulate an electron jumping from the lowest energy core orbital to the highest virtual orbital (orbital 26), JW must apply a string of $Z$-gates across all 25 preceding qubits.\n\nPauli weight = $\\approx 26$. \n\nIn a real quantum algorithm like VQE (Variational Quantum Eigensolver), this creates thousands of sequential CNOT gates. On near-term (NISQ) quantum computers, the hardware noise will destroy the calculation before it finishes.\n\n# Bravyi-Kitaev: \n\nBecause of the binary tree structure, checking the parity of 25 preceding orbitals only requires checking the \"nodes\" that sum up those blocks.\n\nMax Pauli weight = $\\approx \\log_2(26) \\approx \\mathbf{5}$.The Verdict: At $N=26$, BK completely dominates. By squashing a 26-gate operation down to a 5-gate operation, BK drastically reduces the circuit depth, making the exact simulation of water's electronic structure far more feasible on noisy quantum hardware.","x":3960,"y":-4160,"width":760,"height":640},
		{"id":"306b9196d5c7e372","type":"file","file":"pages/chemistry/Example through LiH.md","x":6160,"y":-5180,"width":1400,"height":1720},
		{"id":"bee42f78b0c285bb","type":"text","text":"# AlphaFold2 and Template-Based Methods\n\nAlphaFold2 and RoseTTaFold can handle proteins up to ~2700 residues. Impressive. ","x":-540,"y":806,"width":566,"height":148},
		{"id":"a62987e8dda7c50f","type":"text","text":"# Non-MD ab initio methods (PEP-FOLD3, Quark, Rosetta fragment assembly):\n\n- Hard wall at ~20-50 residues\n- Run times acceptable below this (<14 hours reported)\n- Above this: simply intractable due to search space explosion","x":-1680,"y":1040,"width":566,"height":220},
		{"id":"5deac9a30a391246","type":"text","text":"# All-atom MD (D.E. Shaw's Anton supercomputer, the gold standard)\n\nCan fold proteins accurately, though TC10b (20 residues) required 208 µs of simulation time\nThe authors' own hardware (16 cores + 4 A100 GPUs) generates 725 ns per 24h wall time for a 9092-atom system\nAt that rate, reproducing the Shaw result takes 287 days for a 20-residue peptide\nThat's 10 months for the smallest meaningful benchmark. For 80-residue proteins it becomes completely impractical.","x":-1680,"y":1300,"width":566,"height":284},
		{"id":"5fd207cae5d93e63","type":"text","text":"# REMD (Replica Exchange MD) \nimproves sampling by running many parallel simulations at different temperatures and doing Monte Carlo swaps between neighboring replicas. Prevents low-temperature runs from getting trapped in local minima. But you need tens of replicas even for a 20-residue protein — you multiply compute cost by replica count, partially canceling the benefit.","x":-1680,"y":1620,"width":566,"height":240},
		{"id":"17d2a02e0c720b2c","type":"file","file":"pages/chemistry/Application of Computational Chemistry - Eigenvalues.md","x":1940,"y":-3028,"width":820,"height":680},
		{"id":"dda67d5e8bd8011e","type":"file","file":"pages/chemistry/Application of Computational Chemistry - Eigenstates.md","x":1920,"y":-2220,"width":820,"height":851},
		{"id":"40ab7bfa0092079b","type":"text","text":"> classical ab initio methods have a hard complexity wall around 50 residues for practical use. ","x":-1020,"y":847,"width":440,"height":107},
		{"id":"c67263a4e896daf9","type":"file","file":"pages/chemistry/Application of Computational Chemistry - Eigenbasis.md","x":1920,"y":-1308,"width":840,"height":820},
		{"id":"503cbefa22cada83","type":"file","file":"pages/chemistry/Applications of Eigenproblems Computational Chemistry.md","x":440,"y":-2028,"width":960,"height":480},
		{"id":"6d6a8611e4a59608","type":"text","text":"# ML-accelerated MD (neural network potentials)\nTrain neural nets on all-atom trajectories to replace the force field evaluation. Faster per step, but requires long all-atom runs to generate training data first. Not generalizable — can't extrapolate to sequences far from the training set. One promising approach (ML x MELD x MD) uses ML-derived residue-residue contacts as spatial restraints, successfully predicted up to 200-residue structures, but still requires the classical training infrastructure","x":-1680,"y":1900,"width":566,"height":280},
		{"id":"04bf5c1f18fea963","type":"text","text":"# Coarse-grain conformation search\n1. Represent protein as a chain on a tetrahedral lattice (4 possible turn directions per bond).\n2. Score conformations using Miyazawa-Jernigan (MJ) potentials — a 20×20 empirical matrix of pairwise amino acid interaction energies, derived from contact frequencies in known PDB structures. This is your classical prior injected into the quantum cost function.\n3. Formulate as an Ising Hamiltonian: binary variables encode turn sequences, quadratic and higher-order terms encode MJ interaction energies for residue pairs brought into contact by each conformation.\n4. Run VQE with RealAmplitudes ansatz on IBM_Cleveland (real hardware, not simulation)","x":-1060,"y":2176,"width":506,"height":420},
		{"id":"20bf114d66e21e26","type":"text","text":"1. Coarse-grain conformation search.\n2. Convert output to PDB format.\n3. Reconstruct all-atom structure.\n4. Refinement","x":-1680,"y":2315,"width":507,"height":143},
		{"id":"262d16e4a10ab3ed","type":"file","file":"media-attachments/Pasted image 20260602091218.png","x":-420,"y":2207,"width":710,"height":359},
		{"id":"70468e39e84eb382","type":"text","text":"# MSA Depth Problem\ndata dependency of deep learning methods.\n\nAlphaFold2's Evoformer transformer learns **statistical couplings** between residue positions from the MSA. No MSA = no couplings = the model is essentially guessing based only on local sequence context, with no long-range structural constraints. The whole coevolutionary signal that makes AlphaFold2 powerful simply doesn't exist for orphan proteins.","x":-120,"y":1520,"width":566,"height":280},
		{"id":"651dc789f7f0b8b8","type":"text","text":"VQE beats brute force on the same Hamiltonian. The exhaustive search found RMSD 1.88 Å, VQE found 1.78 Å. This seems paradoxical — brute force finds the true ground state, VQE is heuristic. The explanation: there are multiple local minima close in energy (Figure 4a pattern). The true energy minimum isn't necessarily the structure closest to the experimental crystal structure. VQE's heuristic sampling of near-optimal conformations happened to land on one that's geometrically more accurate, even though it's not the energy minimum. This is actually a feature, not a bug — the free energy landscape has multiple minima and the crystal structure corresponds to one of them, not necessarily the global energy minimum.","x":446,"y":2241,"width":710,"height":290},
		{"id":"d6aa944825a4447b","type":"text","text":"# Mutations\n\nThe paper demonstrates this with **Trp-cage variants TC10b and TC5b** — 85% sequence identity, 3 point mutations, NMR structures with only 0.8 Å RMSD between them (nearly identical experimentally). But compute their free energy landscapes via MD at their respective melting points:\n\n- TC5b: single clean global minimum at -13.5 kJ/mol, textbook folding funnel\n- TC10b: **two distinct folding pathways**, two separate minima at -9.01 and -9.4 kJ/mol, rugged landscape\n\nSame experimental structure, completely different energy landscape. AlphaFold2 predicts structure from the MSA signal, which is similar for both. But the **dynamic behavior and folding thermodynamics** are different — which matters for drug binding, because drugs interact with conformational ensembles, not static structures. The confidence scores (pLDDT) for mutated residues are known to be unreliable.","x":-120,"y":1020,"width":566,"height":471},
		{"id":"0d20ab5b07ad7833","type":"file","file":"media-attachments/Pasted image 20260609084816.png","x":-4880,"y":1620,"width":1240,"height":1584},
		{"id":"b7058709f713581a","type":"file","file":"pages/chemistry/Time Evolution Problem.md","x":4000,"y":-1995,"width":517,"height":248},
		{"id":"25700cf6908e144b","type":"file","file":"pages/chemistry/Thermal Average.md","x":4000,"y":-1675,"width":517,"height":255},
		{
			"id":"96b7fcf184205841",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"# Quantum Algorithms for Quantum Chemistry and Quantum Materials Science",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":5980,
			"y":-2020,
			"width":555,
			"height":100
		},
		{
			"id":"7eddd57f56993539",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"# Protein Structure Prediction Using Quantum Computers\n\n",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":5982,
			"y":320,
			"width":555,
			"height":110
		},
		{
			"id":"78653f7588e60bc1",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"# The Hardness Argument: Two Languages, Same Phenomenon\n\nDoga et al. characterize PSP hardness through three empirical axes: sequence length (conformational explosion), mutations (energy landscape ruggedness), and shallow MSA depth (coevolutionary information poverty).\n\nBauer et al. characterize quantum chemistry hardness through: system size (Hilbert space exponential scaling), strong electron correlation (multireference character), and the distinction between BQP-tractable dynamics and QMA-hard ground states.\n\nThese are the same phenomenon described in different vocabularies. The rugged folding funnel of a mutated, low-MSA protein maps directly onto a multireference quantum chemistry problem: multiple low-energy configurations exist close in energy but structurally distinct, no single dominant conformation exists, and mean-field starting points fail. Both papers identify this regime — rugged, ill-conditioned, high-uncertainty energy landscapes — as the natural home for quantum advantage.\n\nSynthesis insight: Doga et al.'s \"quantum utility subspace\" (small, mutated, low-MSA proteins) is essentially a macroscopic fingerprint of Bauer et al.'s multireference regime applied to biomolecular conformational spaces. The MSA depth proxy for quantum amenability is a practical stand-in for what would formally be the multireference character of the protein's Hamiltonian.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":10060,
			"y":-2020,
			"width":920,
			"height":460
		},
		{
			"id":"51188ae6f09ce589",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"# The Algorithm Stack: VQE as the Bridge\n\nBoth papers converge on VQE as the near-term algorithm of choice, but for different reasons and at different levels of sophistication.\n\nBauer et al. analyze VQE rigorously: they discuss ansatz families (UCC, hardware-efficient, Adapt-VQE, tensor networks), identify the two core bottlenecks (measurement scaling as 1/ε² versus QPE's 1/ε, and barren plateau optimization landscapes), and frame VQE as the NISQ-era approximation to QPE. Crucially, they note VQE is most suitable when circuit depth must be kept short and when systematic coherent errors are acceptable.\n\nDoga et al. deploy VQE in practice using the RealAmplitudes ansatz on IBM hardware for the Miyazawa-Jernigan Hamiltonian. They observe an important empirical phenomenon: VQE's heuristic solution (1.78 Å RMSD) beat the classically-solved exact ground state (1.88 Å RMSD). This isn't a failure — it's VQE doing what Bauer et al. predict it should do in rugged landscapes: sampling near-optimal conformations that happen to be structurally closer to experiment than the mathematical ground state.\n\nSynthesis insight: Bauer et al.'s theoretical framework explains Doga et al.'s empirical result. The Zika P-loop energy landscape has multiple near-degenerate local minima (akin to a multiminima folding funnel), and VQE's stochastic sampling naturally explores these rather than collapsing to a single \"wrong\" minimum. This is precisely the regime where VQE outperforms exhaustive classical search — not by finding the lowest energy, but by sampling low-energy conformations that are structurally diverse.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":10060,
			"y":-1540,
			"width":920,
			"height":540
		},
		{
			"id":"aafb8aaa09e0eadf",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"# The Hamiltonian Problem: Coarse-Grain vs. Ab Initio\n\nThis is the sharpest point of tension between the two papers.\n\nDoga et al. use the Miyazawa-Jernigan (MJ) statistical potential as the cost Hamiltonian. This is a purely empirical, coarse-grained energy function: pairwise residue contact energies derived from statistical analysis of known protein structures, operating on a Cα-only tetrahedral lattice backbone. It is effective and computationally tractable for NISQ hardware, but it encodes no quantum physics.\n\nBauer et al. argue that the ultimate goal of quantum chemistry is to replace exactly these kinds of empirical potentials with first-principles calculations — simulating exact electron dynamics using the ab initio electronic Hamiltonian, potentially with DFT or Hartree-Fock as starting points. Their \"long-term vision\" for molecular force fields is to eliminate the empiricism that currently limits accuracy.\n\nThere is a clear algorithmic roadmap connecting these two papers. The near-term quantum PSP workflow (Doga et al.) uses classical statistical potentials because NISQ hardware cannot support the circuit depths required for ab initio Hamiltonians. As hardware scales toward fault tolerance and QPE becomes viable, the MJ potential could be progressively replaced by quantum-computed interaction energies — first at the level of active-site fragments (Bauer et al.'s quantum embedding / DMFT approach), then full residue-residue interactions. The MJ Hamiltonian is not the destination; it is a computationally tractable proxy that keeps the conformational search quantum while deferring the electron-structure problem to classical methods.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":10060,
			"y":-980,
			"width":920,
			"height":540
		},
		{
			"id":"f7dc25a987449e21",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"#  The Resource Gap: NISQ Limitations\n\nBauer et al. are explicit that current resource estimates for quantum chemistry simulations are \"many orders of magnitude greater than what can be achieved with contemporary hardware.\" QPE — the algorithm needed for chemical accuracy — requires deep circuits and fault tolerance that does not yet exist.\n\nDoga et al. acknowledge the same gap but approach it differently: rather than waiting for fault-tolerant hardware, they identify the problem subspace (small, mutated, low-MSA proteins) where NISQ hardware already provides results competitive with classical methods, even without error correction.\n\nSynthesis insight: The two papers represent two different philosophies for navigating the resource gap. Bauer et al. take the long view: define the ideal algorithm (QPE + ab initio Hamiltonian), estimate resources, and wait for hardware to mature. Doga et al. take the pragmatic view: find the problem instances where NISQ hardware is already useful and build toward fault tolerance incrementally. These are complementary strategies, not competing ones. The PSP workflow could serve as a concrete benchmarking domain for tracking quantum hardware progress — a domain where the answer is experimentally verifiable (crystal structures exist), the problem scales continuously with hardware capability (protein size tracks with qubit count quadratically), and the classical baseline is well-characterized (CASP benchmarks).",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":11040,
			"y":-980,
			"width":880,
			"height":540
		},
		{
			"id":"3cd38e3fb49f78bd",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"# The Complexity Gap: BQP vs. QMA in PSP\n\nBauer et al. draw a critical complexity-theoretic distinction: quantum dynamics (time evolution) is BQP-tractable — quantum computers can simulate it efficiently. Ground-state determination is QMA-hard — there is no general polynomial-time quantum algorithm.\n\nDoga et al. frame PSP as an optimization problem (find the minimum-energy conformation) and implicitly treat it as a search problem amenable to Grover/QAOA speedups. But the exact ground-state problem on the MJ Hamiltonian is, formally, an NP-hard combinatorial optimization on a discrete lattice — and QMA contains NP.\n\nSynthesis insight: Doga et al.'s claim of quantum advantage rests not on solving the ground state exactly, but on heuristic sampling in the near-optimal energy regime. This is the correct framing when viewed through Bauer et al.'s lens: the quantum advantage for PSP is not a complexity-theoretic guarantee but an empirical one, arising from the favorable geometry of quantum search in rugged landscapes. The paper's observation that VQE sampling can outperform exact classical optimization is consistent with this — it is sampling low-energy configurations rather than minimizing exactly. This means the relevant complexity class for PSP quantum advantage is not QMA (ground state) but something closer to the practical heuristic performance of VQE on near-multireference landscapes.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":11040,
			"y":-2020,
			"width":880,
			"height":460
		},
		{
			"id":"b55d463d3c1bb27e",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"# The Encoding Problem: A Missing Bridge\n\nBauer et al. devote significant attention to qubit representations: first vs. second quantization, Jordan-Wigner vs. Bravyi-Kitaev mappings, Gaussian vs. plane-wave basis sets. These choices dramatically affect circuit depth, gate count, and Hamiltonian sparsity.\n\nDoga et al. encode the problem in a tetrahedral lattice (four discrete turn directions per residue) with binary variables for the conformational degrees of freedom. This is essentially a first-quantization lattice model — each residue occupies a lattice site, and the Hamiltonian is the sum of pairwise MJ interactions.\n\nSynthesis insight: The lattice encoding Doga et al. use is a natural fit for the \"model Hamiltonian\" regime Bauer et al. discuss (section 3.2.4) — where the Hamiltonian is specified directly on a lattice rather than derived from ab initio integrals. Bauer et al. note that lattice models permit specialized Fermionic encodings and that developing representations for model problems is \"of particular importance in simulating condensed matter systems.\" The tetrahedral lattice protein model is structurally identical to a lattice spin model, and the same qubit efficiency techniques (Bravyi-Kitaev, symmetry-adapted mappings) could in principle be applied to compress the qubit register beyond the straightforward dense encoding Doga et al. use. This is an unexploited optimization in the PSP quantum workflow.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":11040,
			"y":-1540,
			"width":880,
			"height":540
		},
		{
			"id":"01bc1302e94fd6be",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"# The Embedding Bridge: Quantum Chemistry Inside PSP\n\nBauer et al. extensively discuss quantum embedding methods (DMFT, DMET, QM/MM) as the near-term strategy for handling large systems: solve a small, strongly correlated quantum subsystem on the quantum computer while treating the environment classically.\n\nDoga et al. mention that enzyme active sites (catalytic reactions in enzymes) are one potential application of their PSP framework, and note that a quantum Hamiltonian could simulate the catalytic active site while classical force fields handle the protein backbone.\n\nSynthesis insight: This is the most direct point of contact between the two papers. The Bauer et al. quantum embedding framework (DMFT/DMET + VQE for the impurity problem) could be embedded inside the Doga et al. PSP workflow. Concretely: use VQE on a tetrahedral lattice to predict the Cα backbone conformation (Doga), then use quantum embedding with the full electronic Hamiltonian to compute accurate binding energies at the active site (Bauer). This two-level hybrid — coarse-grain quantum conformational search + fine-grain quantum electronic structure — is exactly the kind of multiscale quantum-classical workflow both papers independently argue toward.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":11940,
			"y":-2020,
			"width":740,
			"height":460
		},
		{
			"id":"a74e0fa1a46c5121",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Ruggedness is the unifying concept. Multireference character (Bauer) and low-MSA / mutated protein landscapes (Doga) are the same phenomenon at different scales. Both identify ruggedness as the regime where quantum advantage is most accessible.\n\nVQE's empirical advantage over exact classical optimization in rugged landscapes is theoretically predicted by Bauer et al. and empirically confirmed by Doga et al.\n\nThe MJ potential is a necessary interim step, not the destination. The roadmap leads from statistical potentials toward ab initio Hamiltonians as hardware scales.\n\nThe lattice encoding used in PSP is formally equivalent to a model Hamiltonian on a lattice — meaning all of Bauer et al.'s efficiency techniques (Bravyi-Kitaev, symmetry-adapted mappings, divide-and-conquer Hamiltonian simulation) are in principle applicable.\n\nQuantum embedding is the natural bridge between the two papers: VQE conformational search at the backbone level + ab initio quantum chemistry at the active site level defines a concrete near-term hybrid workflow.\n\nPSP is a uniquely well-suited benchmarking domain for tracking quantum hardware progress: experimentally verifiable, continuously scalable, and with a strong classical baseline (CASP).",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":10990,
			"y":-380,
			"width":980,
			"height":520
		},
		{
			"id":"0d952dcf009d4a06",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"## Benchmark Systems\n\nUtilizing classical verification standards (e.g., density functional theory, coupled cluster limits, quantum Monte Carlo) to establish a rigorous baseline for true quantum advantage.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":6599,
			"y":-20,
			"width":581,
			"height":160
		},
		{
			"id":"fa3a9e61a01195f4",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"## Data Readout Methods\nExtracting targeted physical insights through equal-time expectation measurements or time-dependent Green's functions",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":7200,
			"y":-20,
			"width":381,
			"height":160
		},
		{
			"id":"3cf9d539b32b415e",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"# Evaluation and Readout",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":6554,
			"y":-140,
			"width":210,
			"height":80
		},
		{
			"id":"6ae73bddf4fc251d",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"## Variational Quantum Eigensolvers\nImplements a hybrid quantum-classical optimization feedback loop to iteratively lower trial state energies on near-term hardware.\n\nAnsatz Typologies: Unitary Coupled Cluster (UCC), Hardware-Efficient structures, dynamic Adapt-VQE pooling, and Tensor Networks (MPS, MERA, deep MERA).\n\nPrimary Bottlenecks: Sampling noise demands high repetition overhead (errors scale as $1/\\epsilon^2$), alongside classical parameter optimization traps (local minima).",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":7715,
			"y":-640,
			"width":545,
			"height":300
		},
		{
			"id":"7acc84746589fb6a",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"## Quantum Phase Estimation\nUses controlled time-evolution blocks ($e^{-it\\hat{H}}$) to project trial wave functions directly onto exact energy eigenstates.\n\nIterative QPE variations trade multi-qubit measurement registers for intensive classical post-processing.\n\nAchieves precise results scaling as $1/\\epsilon$, making it a core target for future error-corrected, fault-tolerant platforms.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":8280,
			"y":-640,
			"width":500,
			"height":260
		},
		{
			"id":"4bed7c98a016a01d",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"## Hamiltonian Simulation\nProduct Formulas: Standard Trotter-Suzuki mappings step through individual interacting terms, alongside optimized multiproduct formulas that limit circuit depth.\n    \nAdvanced Primitives: Linear Combination of Unitaries (LCU) Taylor expansions, Quantum Walks, and Quantum Signal Processing (QSP) / Qubitization block encodings.\n    \nLocality Exploitation: Divide-and-conquer methodologies leverage Lieb-Robinson bounds to simulate lattice setups linearly with space-time volume.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":8800,
			"y":-640,
			"width":500,
			"height":320
		},
		{
			"id":"6bcbdcb614a23233",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"# Algorithm Architectures & Simulation Primitives",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":6565,
			"y":-780,
			"width":418,
			"height":100
		},
		{
			"id":"02c914d5b3ca72fa",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"## Qubit Representation & Encodings\nFirst Quantization: Uses Configuration Interaction (CI) spaces to map electrons to qubit registers, scaling logarithmically with basis functions in the continuum limit.\n\nSecond Quantization: Tracks orbital occupation numbers across Fock space, natively enforcing Fermi statistics at the operator level.\n\nElectronic Basis Selection: Balances compact wave function representation (Gaussian atomic bases) against structural sparsity and periodic simplicity (Plane waves with pseudopotentials).\n\nFermion-to-Qubit Mappings: Transitions operators to Pauli matrices via frameworks like **Jordan-Wigner** (costly non-local strings) or **Bravyi-Kitaev** (optimizes operation weight to $O(\\log N)$).",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":6610,
			"y":-640,
			"width":570,
			"height":400
		},
		{
			"id":"bb17a07919ad943e",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"## Prescribed-Path State Preparation\n _Adiabatic State Preparation_: Evolves a system slowly along a continuous path; runtime is constrained by the minimum spectral gap ($g_{min}$).\n    \n_Quantum Imaginary-Time Evolution (QITE)_: Avoids optimization loops by projecting ground states using unitaries calculated from localized geometric qubit domains.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":7200,
			"y":-640,
			"width":500,
			"height":240
		},
		{
			"id":"57350983fd109f65",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"## Hybrid Systems & Extensions\nExcited state formulations via Quantum Subspace Expansion (QSE) or Equation-of-Motion (EOM) generalized eigenvalue techniques.\n\nFinite-temperature algorithms designed to compute complex thermal averages.\n\n**Quantum Embedding**: Multiscale partitioning coupling a highly correlated quantum register subsystem to a classically simulated environment (e.g., DMFT).",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":9360,
			"y":-640,
			"width":520,
			"height":320
		},
		{
			"id":"70aed4cab8844c02",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"## Quantum Chemistry\nFocuses on solving low-lying electronic eigenstates under the fixed-nucleus Born-Oppenheimer approximation.\n\nDistinguishes between easily handled _single-reference_ problems (dominant configuration) and strongly correlated _multireference_ setups (multiple configuration interaction).\n\nPrimary targets include metalloenzyme active sites (e.g., nitrogenase, oxygen-evolving complex), transition metal nanocatalysts, and light-harvesting pigments.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":6600,
			"y":-1200,
			"width":580,
			"height":320
		},
		{
			"id":"1909673bf05e15a3",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"## Quantum Molecular Spectroscopy\nAims to compute the highly anharmonic eigenstates of the nuclear Schrödinger equation to interpret rovibrational transitions.\n\nExemplified by highly fluxional \"floppy\" molecules like $\\text{CH}_5^+$ or the moving networks of hydrogen-bonded water clusters.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":7200,
			"y":-1200,
			"width":500,
			"height":220
		},
		{"id":"972b011708b78939","type":"text","text":"## Chemical Quantum Dynamics\nTracks non-equilibrium electronic and nuclear motion subjected to external fields.\n\nAddresses the breakdown of the Born-Oppenheimer approximation at **conical intersections** (nonadiabatic quantum effects).\n\nTargets complex mechanisms such as Proton-Coupled Electron Transfer (PCET), complex condensed-phase environments (Kubo theory limitations), and hot plasmonic chemistry.","x":7720,"y":-1200,"width":540,"height":320},
		{
			"id":"2aca05b18edd49a5",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"## Dynamical Quantum Effects in Materials\nModels out-of-equilibrium realities including mesoscopic electron transport, neutron scattering metrics, and ultrafast pump-probe laser responses.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":8780,
			"y":-1200,
			"width":460,
			"height":320
		},
		{
			"id":"2325bc790ddbbb64",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"## Correlated Electronic Structure in Materials\nStudies strongly interacting systems where effective non-interacting band theories fail, such as **Mott insulators**.\n\nInvestigates macro-scale physics including high-temperature superconductivity phase diagrams, non-Fermi liquids (strange metals), flat-band Moiré materials (twisted bilayer graphene), and frustrated topological spin liquids.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":8280,
			"y":-1200,
			"width":480,
			"height":320
		},
		{
			"id":"b23b6146e95f59a8",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"# Simulation Challenges in Molecular & Materials Science",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":6555,
			"y":-1340,
			"width":418,
			"height":100
		},
		{
			"id":"e741f42eee66e926",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"## Quantum Information Fundamentals",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":6600,
			"y":-1720,
			"width":295,
			"height":80
		},
		{
			"id":"c1763977f999b427",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"## Quantum Complexity Frameworks\n\nReal-time quantum dynamics simulates physical systems efficiently and falls under the BQP (bounded-error quantum polynomial) complexity class.\n\nGround-state determination and finite-temperature thermal averages belong to the harder **QMA** (quantum Merlin-Arthur) complexity class, meaning they lack general polynomial-time guarantees on quantum computers.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":6988,
			"y":-1720,
			"width":532,
			"height":280
		},
		{
			"id":"3fe6ed0204f4c826",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"## Quantum Computing Paradigm Models\n\nAnalogue Quantum Simulation: Utilizing a controllable physical system (such as cold atomic gases in optical lattices) to directly mimic target quantum behavior.\n\nDigital Quantum Simulation: A circuit-based approach mapping instructions into a sequence of discrete, few-qubit quantum gates.\n    \nAdiabatic Quantum Computation: Moving slowly along a time-dependent Hamiltonian path to continuously track the ground state.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":7540,
			"y":-1720,
			"width":560,
			"height":280
		},
		{
			"id":"5bf3f440f49aef35",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"# Introduction & Computational Foundations",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":6555,
			"y":-1860,
			"width":385,
			"height":100
		},
		{
			"id":"a34e75dd11b47fe2",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"## Hardware and Error Realities\n**NISQ (Noisy Intermediate-Scale Quantum) Era**: Current small-scale devices susceptible to qubit decoherence and gate errors.\n\n**Quantum Error Correction (QEC)**: Mitigation of faults by encoding single, error-corrected **logical qubits** into highly entangled arrays of physical qubits (e.g., surface codes), incurring massive hardware overhead.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":8120,
			"y":-1720,
			"width":480,
			"height":280
		},
		{
			"id":"d2f275ac004da4a9",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"## Local disorders\n**Mutations** alter the local chemical space and can dramatically reshape the free energy landscape — even conservative substitutions (e.g. Val → Leu) can introduce new local minima or remove existing ones. AlphaFold2 confidence scores and 3D coordinates degrade significantly for mutated variants.\n\n**Intrinsically Disordered Regions (IDRs)** occupy shallow, multiminima energy landscapes. They exist as structural ensembles, not single conformations. AlphaFold2 predicts them with low pLDDT scores and tends to bias toward a single folded-like conformation, which is often misleading for drug design.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":6424,
			"y":660,
			"width":393,
			"height":460
		},
		{
			"id":"c73b6e024e1d0144",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"# Quantum Utility Subspace\nProteins that are small (few dozen residues), carry point mutations, and have shallow MSAs are the best candidates for quantum algorithms to outperform state-of-the-art classical methods. This subspace grows as quantum hardware scales.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":6424,
			"y":1150,
			"width":393,
			"height":220
		},
		{
			"id":"42d43d04e10c87dc",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"## Shallow MSA Depth\n\nAlphaFold2's Evoformer extracts coevolutionary signals from Multiple Sequence Alignments (MSAs). Accuracy degrades sharply when the effective sequence count (N_eff) drops below ~30, and diminishing returns set in above ~100. Orphan proteins (~20% of metagenomic sequences) have near-zero MSA depth and produce essentially random predictions (CASP15 GDT_TS ~25, equivalent to a random guess).",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":6844,
			"y":660,
			"width":420,
			"height":340
		},
		{
			"id":"c4d1af6109fc27a8",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"## Why PSP is Hard\nThree compounding axes that increase energy landscape ruggedness and defeat classical deep learning methods.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":5998,
			"y":520,
			"width":586,
			"height":120
		},
		{
			"id":"79132f98c80e45f4",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"# Sequence Length\nFor a protein of _n_ amino acids, the conformational search space grows as $3^{(2n−2)}$. Even a 20-residue peptide takes ~10 months of continuous MD simulation on modern HPC to fold. Physics-based (ab initio) methods are generally limited to a few dozen residues.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":5998,
			"y":660,
			"width":406,
			"height":240
		},
		{
			"id":"f4f2718197b7cda4",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"# AlphaFold2 Failure Regimes\n\nThe three axes above converge into a well-defined subspace where DL-based methods perform poorly: mutated variants, orphan proteins, IDPs, and flexible loop regions.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":5998,
			"y":1150,
			"width":406,
			"height":180
		},
		{
			"id":"f6436e302a870691",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"## QAOA / VQE\n\nFrames PSP as Hamiltonian ground-state optimization. A cost Hamiltonian encodes conformational energy; a variational quantum circuit minimizes it. Low-depth QAOA is not expected to beat classical methods, but at higher depth it increasingly approximates Grover speedup. VQE is the specific algorithm used in the paper's experiments.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":7770,
			"y":500,
			"width":460,
			"height":240
		},
		{
			"id":"798d7f0fffa2c803",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"## Ruggedness → Quantum Advantage\n\nA rugged folding funnel (many local minima, high uncertainty) is precisely the regime where quantum tunneling and interference provide the most leverage. The more rugged the landscape, the harder it is for classical heuristics to escape local minima — and the easier it is for a quantum algorithm to tunnel through barriers. Reduced MSA depth produces more rugged Potts models, directly linking hardness to quantum amenability.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":7770,
			"y":760,
			"width":460,
			"height":300
		},
		{
			"id":"3d7d9a2c9c71c8fc",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"## Grover Search\n\nFrames PSP as an unstructured database search over all possible conformers. Grover's algorithm provides a quadratic (√N) speedup over classical exhaustive search. Any quantum search or optimization algorithm can be viewed as a parametrized approximation of Grover's algorithm.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":7314,
			"y":500,
			"width":443,
			"height":240
		},
		{
			"id":"e43f1d6e3dbe60e0",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"## Miyazawa-Jernigan Statistical Potential\n\nUsed as the cost Hamiltonian. Provides pairwise residue–residue interaction energies for all 20 amino acid combinations, derived from statistical analysis of known protein structures. More expressive than the simple HP (hydrophobic-polar) model, and computationally tractable for coarse-grain quantum circuits.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":7314,
			"y":760,
			"width":443,
			"height":300
		},
		{
			"id":"672bb83188b36493",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"# Hybrid Workflow\n\nQuantum computers are not used end-to-end — only the computationally hardest step (conformational search) runs on quantum hardware.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":5998,
			"y":1460,
			"width":660,
			"height":120
		},
		{
			"id":"148670f5563bb0a0",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"# VQE on Quantum Hardware\n\nThe protein backbone is represented as a coarse-grain Cα-only chain on a tetrahedral lattice (four possible turn directions per residue). VQE minimizes the Miyazawa-Jernigan Ising Hamiltonian to find the lowest-energy lattice conformation. Run on `IBM_Cleveland` (127-qubit Eagle).",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":5998,
			"y":1620,
			"width":406,
			"height":260
		},
		{
			"id":"4c65f0ab0f9c1b3e",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"# Classical Rebuild\n\nThe quantum output (xyz coordinates) is converted to a PDB file, then sidechains are placed classically to reconstruct the full all-atom structure. The backbone geometry from the quantum step is preserved throughout.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":6424,
			"y":1620,
			"width":380,
			"height":260
		},
		{
			"id":"eb2562f7bc4e251d",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"# Molecular Mechanics Refinement\n\nA classical force-field energy minimization relaxes the all-atom structure beyond the discrete lattice constraints, allowing finer geometric optimization.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":6824,
			"y":1620,
			"width":360,
			"height":260
		},
		{
			"id":"5ebb932a6b4d0581",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"#  Zika NS3 P-loop (LHPGAGK)\n\nSeven-residue catalytic P-loop from the Zika virus NS3 helicase, a drug discovery target. Results:\n\n|Method|Backbone RMSD vs crystal|\n|---|---|\n|PEP-FOLD3|1.64 Å|\n|VQE (IBM_Cleveland)|1.78 Å|\n|Classical brute force + Gurobi|1.88 Å|\n|AlphaFold2|3.53 Å|\n\nVQE's heuristic solution beat the classically-solved ground state — because multiple near-degenerate local minima exist, and VQE happened to sample the one structurally closer to the crystal structure.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":5998,
			"y":1920,
			"width":660,
			"height":420
		},
		{
			"id":"ea62d3e3defd4f35",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"# Resource Estimation\nQubit count scales quadratically with sequence length (both configuration and interaction qubits).\n\n| Hardware       | Qubits | Max residues (estimated) |\n| -------------- | ------ | ------------------------ |\n| IBM Eagle (R3) | 127    | ~22                      |\n| IBM Osprey     | 433    | ~41                      |\n| IBM Condor     | 1121   | ~67                      |\nWith 10,000 qubits and sufficient gate fidelity, biomedically relevant proteins like hemoglobin (141 residues, ~4,967 entangled qubits estimated) could become tractable.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":7314,
			"y":1460,
			"width":486,
			"height":400
		},
		{
			"id":"3210225aae0fe281",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"# Long-Term Vision\n\nQuantum and classical methods are framed as synergistic, not competing. Near-term quantum algorithms will mirror classical physics-based methods (lattice models, statistical potentials), eventually progressing toward quantum chemical calculations (DFT, Hartree-Fock) that could simulate exact electron dynamics. The ultimate goal is solving the path-dependent protein folding problem itself — not just structure prediction.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":7314,
			"y":1880,
			"width":726,
			"height":220
		},
		{"id":"28f68d0fb40e1486","type":"file","file":"media-attachments/quantum-algorithms-for-quantum-chemistry-and-quantum-materials-science.pdf","x":8680,"y":-1990,"width":585,"height":550}
	],
	"edges":[
		{"id":"ee3f50db73cd500a","fromNode":"7216579fd61aa44f","fromSide":"right","toNode":"16ec7d5baa1b9a49","toSide":"left","label":"Solving through \nQuantum Algorithms is characterized"},
		{"id":"81dd8458211f27a8","fromNode":"95cf4b50dfb4c4ac","fromSide":"right","toNode":"28732cfcfc7325ec","toSide":"left","label":"is solved by"},
		{"id":"60a5e98ac2fb3c8d","fromNode":"a50c92e62d6e4777","fromSide":"right","toNode":"28732cfcfc7325ec","toSide":"left","label":"is used to solve"},
		{"id":"bd51fbc58e44d502","fromNode":"13c7a2ec23b55a4d","fromSide":"right","toNode":"95cf4b50dfb4c4ac","toSide":"left","label":"is the core nature of \nthe problem of"},
		{"id":"f628181cffbc6169","fromNode":"28732cfcfc7325ec","fromSide":"bottom","toNode":"a0b9d01853144473","toSide":"top","label":"at minimum, any problem\nuses a subset of"},
		{"id":"a5181c7081a40439","fromNode":"9684a51b07a5051b","fromSide":"right","toNode":"95cf4b50dfb4c4ac","toSide":"left","label":"is the main \nmethod to solve"},
		{"id":"b5b6647beb5a3fd1","fromNode":"537c63440818939a","fromSide":"right","toNode":"7216579fd61aa44f","toSide":"left"},
		{"id":"9a45fbc2264c3841","fromNode":"28732cfcfc7325ec","fromSide":"top","toNode":"01066344b3b34b81","toSide":"bottom","label":"is mapped to"},
		{"id":"af0d4f13575944f6","fromNode":"28732cfcfc7325ec","fromSide":"right","toNode":"503cbefa22cada83","toSide":"left","label":"can be used for"},
		{"id":"32502a649fb4b02f","fromNode":"503cbefa22cada83","fromSide":"right","toNode":"17d2a02e0c720b2c","toSide":"left","label":"in how a reaction\nwill result"},
		{"id":"ada22a91caf9e7f9","fromNode":"503cbefa22cada83","fromSide":"right","toNode":"dda67d5e8bd8011e","toSide":"left","label":"in what is the geometry \nof the molecule"},
		{"id":"41865db6504fc5c9","fromNode":"503cbefa22cada83","fromSide":"right","toNode":"c67263a4e896daf9","toSide":"left","label":"in how reactive and stable it is"},
		{"id":"0163eacb42641dd0","fromNode":"16ec7d5baa1b9a49","fromSide":"top","toNode":"f3907f726fad4e46","toSide":"bottom","label":"the Hamiltonian is derived from"},
		{"id":"0de03267745e742f","fromNode":"f3907f726fad4e46","fromSide":"top","toNode":"4c5a71419fc81684","toSide":"bottom","label":"Example of Bit and \nQubit Scale"},
		{"id":"d39f34f0492d6afa","fromNode":"f3907f726fad4e46","fromSide":"top","toNode":"306b9196d5c7e372","toSide":"bottom","label":"Example of \nEncoding and Solving"},
		{"id":"26ca6979665d7680","fromNode":"4c5a71419fc81684","fromSide":"left","toNode":"23033b6bb71db598","toSide":"right"},
		{"id":"96ad560f00654cf9","fromNode":"6ec17e1bfe742169","fromSide":"bottom","toNode":"8c538aa7d7562042","toSide":"left"},
		{"id":"21da8275656cdc53","fromNode":"6ec17e1bfe742169","fromSide":"bottom","toNode":"d8f095e6c4567f25","toSide":"left"},
		{"id":"05d9b9d96202074c","fromNode":"8c538aa7d7562042","fromSide":"right","toNode":"55fe6b5fa878befc","toSide":"left"},
